{
  // The browser suite's TypeScript project (Task 1.13.2).
  //
  // It extends the shared base like every other package, and adds four things,
  // each of which is here for a reason worth reading before changing it.
  //
  // **`noEmit`, and it is safe here for the same reason it is safe in
  // `apps/frontend`.** This package is referenced by nothing, so no consumer
  // typechecks against declarations it would have produced. `composite` still
  // applies alongside it and is still required: without it the root solution
  // file cannot reference this project at all.
  //
  // **`lib` includes DOM, and `types` includes node, and both are true at
  // once** — which is unusual here and is a property of what a spec is. The
  // spec file itself runs in **Node**, under Playwright's runner; the code
  // inside `page.evaluate()` runs in the **browser**. So a spec legitimately
  // needs `document` and `process` in the same file, which is exactly the
  // combination `apps/frontend`'s browser boundary exists to forbid. The
  // asymmetry is deliberate rather than an oversight: nothing in this package
  // is bundled, shipped or served, so a Node global reaching it cannot leak
  // into a page.
  //
  // **`include` covers `playwright.config.ts` as well as the specs**, which is
  // what keeps that file out of `eslint.config.mjs`'s trailing
  // `disableTypeChecked` block. Every one of the seven files in that block is a
  // config file sitting *outside* its package's tsconfig `include`; this one is
  // inside, so the project service has a program for it and it is linted
  // type-aware like the specs. Verified rather than assumed — see
  // BROWSER-TESTING.md.
  "extends": "../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "lib": ["es2024", "dom", "dom.iterable"],
    "types": ["node"]
  },

  // The whole package, not `specs/**/*`. See above.
  "include": ["**/*.ts"],

  // The specs assert on `BACKEND_STATUSES` and the rest of Story 1.12's
  // vocabulary rather than writing the words out by hand, which is the reason
  // Task 1.13.1 rejected a bare root-level directory: pnpm links a workspace
  // package only into packages that declare it, so a specs home that could not
  // declare this dependency would have forced exactly the duplication
  // `packages/shared` exists to prevent.
  "references": [{ "path": "../packages/shared" }]
}
